.TH NTP-SYNC 1 "2026-11-05" "ntp-sync 0.2" "N900 tools"
.SH NAME
ntp-sync \- set the clock over NTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntp-sync
[\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] [\fB\-h\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the system clock once with the busybox \fBntpd\fR and then writes it to the hardware clock
(\fBhwclock \-w\fR). Needs root. The Maemo 5 setting for automatic time uses the cellular network
time (NITZ), not NTP, so it does nothing without a SIM card. A correct clock is needed to verify
TLS certificates.
.PP
The script is also called by \fI/etc/network/if-up.d/ntp-sync\fR when a network interface comes up,
at most once every 10 minutes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Only query the servers and print the offset, do not change the clock.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet, print nothing (used by the network hook).
.TP
.B \-h
Print a short help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ntp-sync.conf
NTP servers, one host name per line. Lines starting with # are ignored.
.TP
.I /var/run/ntp-sync.stamp
Time of the last run of the network hook.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NTP_SERVERS
Space separated list of servers, overrides the configuration file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if no server answered within 60 seconds or the user is not root, 2 for a wrong option.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ntp-sync \-n          # show the offset only
sudo gainroot; ntp-sync
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBntpd\fR(8), \fBhwclock\fR(8)
